WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.942
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.942 --> 00:00:02.354
Friday the thirtieth.

00:00:02.354 --> 00:00:10.088
Calendar's front-loaded, done with work by early afternoon, and then there's a late movie on there to close it out.

00:00:10.088 --> 00:00:12.577
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:12.577 --> 00:00:18.765
If you double the distance between a light and your subject, how much of that light is left?

00:00:18.765 --> 00:00:20.110
Answer's at the end.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:21.724
Weather's mostly a gift.

00:00:21.724 --> 00:00:30.736
Sunny and dry, up near seventy, but the northwest wind gusts into the mid-twenties this afternoon, so expect some shoving on the bike.

00:00:30.736 --> 00:00:32.754
Tonight drops to around fifty.

00:00:32.754 --> 00:00:33.696
Okay, the day.

00:00:33.696 --> 00:00:37.126
Calendar has the gym at seven, straight sets again.

00:00:37.126 --> 00:00:42.910
Then maker space on there from eight to quarter to nine, so that's a tight turnaround.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:45.667
Godinger is only a half day, nine to one.

00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:47.819
After that, nothing until night.

00:00:47.819 --> 00:00:54.881
There's a movie with friends on there, downtown at the Alamo, doors at eight-thirty and the show at nine.

00:00:54.881 --> 00:00:58.581
So the whole afternoon's open, and it'll be a late one.

00:00:58.581 --> 00:00:59.522
Alright, news.

00:00:59.522 --> 00:01:00.935
One from your corner.

00:01:00.935 --> 00:01:06.248
ElevenLabs put out a new version of its sound effects model, SFX version three.

00:01:06.248 --> 00:01:09.880
You type what you want to hear and it makes the sound.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:11.696
The upgrade is in layering.

00:01:11.696 --> 00:01:22.255
It's better at stacking several sounds into one clip, like rain on a window with traffic underneath and a door closing, instead of making them one at a time.

00:01:22.255 --> 00:01:29.115
If you ever want a little bed of sound under a product video, that's the kind of thing it's built for.

00:01:29.115 --> 00:01:31.604
Further away, but it hits gas prices.

00:01:31.604 --> 00:01:40.011
Iranian officials say three oil tankers caught fire after hitting sea mines while trying to get through the Strait of Hormuz.

00:01:40.011 --> 00:01:44.853
That's the narrow channel a big chunk of the world's oil passes through.

00:01:44.853 --> 00:01:51.108
Iran's foreign minister says he's talking with mediators about a seven-day plan to reopen it.

00:01:51.108 --> 00:01:54.807
Until that happens, expect energy prices to stay jumpy.

00:01:54.807 --> 00:01:56.354
And one closer to home.

00:01:56.354 --> 00:01:59.851
New York's minimum wage won't go up in the new year.

00:01:59.851 --> 00:02:07.048
A rule from twenty twenty-three freezes it in any year private sector jobs shrink, and this year qualified.

00:02:07.048 --> 00:02:12.832
The governor says she wants to revisit it next year, but for now, no raise in January.

00:02:12.832 --> 00:02:15.119
Today's challenge is a making one.

00:02:15.119 --> 00:02:20.499
With the afternoon free, fix one small broken thing you've been stepping around.

00:02:20.499 --> 00:02:25.611
A wobbly chair, a drawer that sticks, a loose handle, a light that flickers.

00:02:25.611 --> 00:02:28.234
Pick the one that's bugged you longest.

00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:31.058
Twenty minutes, whatever tools you've got.

00:02:31.058 --> 00:02:35.699
It's a small thing, but every time you use it after, it'll feel good.

00:02:35.699 --> 00:02:38.188
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:02:38.188 --> 00:02:43.635
Remember setting up the memo's audio, and the target of about minus sixteen LUFS?

00:02:43.635 --> 00:02:46.729
Here's what that number is actually measuring.

00:02:46.729 --> 00:02:50.361
LUFS stands for loudness units relative to full scale.

00:02:50.361 --> 00:02:53.589
Full scale is the loudest a digital file can go.

00:02:53.589 --> 00:02:58.365
So every reading is a negative number, counting down from that ceiling.

00:02:58.365 --> 00:03:01.189
Minus sixteen is louder than minus twenty.

00:03:01.189 --> 00:03:07.982
The clever part is that it tries to measure loudness the way your ear does, not the way a meter does.

00:03:07.982 --> 00:03:11.345
Your ears aren't equally sensitive to every pitch.

00:03:11.345 --> 00:03:19.214
Deep bass takes a lot of energy to sound loud, while the middle range, where voices live, sounds loud with much less.

00:03:19.214 --> 00:03:22.106
So the measurement filters the audio first.

00:03:22.106 --> 00:03:28.092
It turns down the very low end and gives a small boost up top, roughly how hearing works.

00:03:28.092 --> 00:03:30.110
Then it skips the quiet parts.

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:39.055
Gaps between sentences and near-silence get left out of the average, so a pause-heavy episode doesn't read as quieter than it sounds.

00:03:39.055 --> 00:03:43.427
What's left is averaged across the whole episode into one number.

00:03:43.427 --> 00:03:46.319
That's why it's called integrated loudness.

00:03:46.319 --> 00:03:47.529
Why minus sixteen?

00:03:47.529 --> 00:03:56.811
It's the common podcast target, loud enough to hear over a bus or a bike lane on a phone, with room left so speech doesn't sound squashed.

00:03:56.811 --> 00:04:01.519
Music apps like Spotify normalize a bit louder, around minus fourteen.

00:04:01.519 --> 00:04:03.604
And there's one more guardrail.

00:04:03.604 --> 00:04:13.087
The tallest peaks get kept about one decibel under the ceiling, because turning audio into MP3 can push peaks a little higher than they were.

00:04:13.087 --> 00:04:19.342
So: calendar says gym at seven, maker space at eight, Godinger till one, and a movie at nine.

00:04:19.342 --> 00:04:20.888
And fix that one thing.

00:04:20.888 --> 00:04:24.453
The answer: about a quarter, which is two full stops.

00:04:24.453 --> 00:04:26.000
Have a good lift, Ziad.
